WEBVTT

00:00:00.470 --> 00:00:04.495
Threat modeling an MCP system means separating the instruction path, the data path,

00:00:04.495 --> 00:00:05.760
and the action path.

00:00:05.760 --> 00:00:09.760
The instruction path includes user prompts, system instructions, tool descriptions,

00:00:09.760 --> 00:00:13.220
prompt templates, and external content that may influence model behavior.

00:00:13.220 --> 00:00:17.695
The data path includes resources, files, retrieval results, tool responses, logs,

00:00:17.695 --> 00:00:19.380
and downstream records.

00:00:19.380 --> 00:00:22.835
The action path includes tool calls that can read, write, send, modify, approve,

00:00:22.835 --> 00:00:25.140
or trigger activity in other systems.

00:00:25.140 --> 00:00:28.810
Prompt injection is a high-level risk where untrusted instructions influence model

00:00:28.810 --> 00:00:31.520
behavior in a way the system did not intend.

00:00:31.520 --> 00:00:35.170
Indirect prompt injection is especially relevant because the model may read external

00:00:35.170 --> 00:00:36.470
content from documents,

00:00:36.470 --> 00:00:41.520
tickets, web pages, emails, repositories, or resources exposed through MCP.

00:00:41.520 --> 00:00:43.435
The training point is not to memorize payloads.

00:00:43.435 --> 00:00:47.625
The stable lesson is that external content should be treated as data with provenance and

00:00:47.625 --> 00:00:47.910
policy,

00:00:47.910 --> 00:00:51.395
not as trusted instructions that can override application rules.

00:00:51.395 --> 00:00:53.160
Tool metadata can also create risk.

00:00:53.160 --> 00:00:57.370
A malicious or misleading tool description may shape when the model chooses a tool or what

00:00:57.370 --> 00:00:58.375
arguments it sends.

00:00:58.375 --> 00:01:01.600
Overbroad permissions can turn a narrow task into broad access.

00:01:01.600 --> 00:01:05.330
Confused deputy risk appears when a component with legitimate authority is tricked into

00:01:05.330 --> 00:01:07.821
using that authority for an unintended purpose.

00:01:07.821 --> 00:01:10.700
Supply-chain risk appears when a server, dependency, package,

00:01:10.700 --> 00:01:13.640
or installation path is trusted without enough review.

00:01:13.640 --> 00:01:17.920
A practical MCP threat model asks three blunt questions: what can the agent read,

00:01:17.920 --> 00:01:19.920
what can it call, and what can it change?

00:01:19.920 --> 00:01:23.090
Then it tests the answers against misuse scenarios such as data leakage,

00:01:23.090 --> 00:01:27.775
unauthorized actions, stale or untrusted resources, malicious server registration,

00:01:27.775 --> 00:01:29.940
over-trusting model output, and approval bypass.

00:01:29.940 --> 00:01:34.504
The resulting controls should live outside the model: authorization checks, allowlists,

00:01:34.504 --> 00:01:37.520
validation, approvals, isolation, logging, and incident response.
